<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Review problems on gradients and directional derivatives
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-exercises-gradients" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Exercises on Gradients and Directional Derivatives</title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems use the gradient to compute directional derivatives and directions of fastest change. If you would like to review this topic first, see <xref ref="section-directional-derivatives" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="rev-gr-1">
<statement>
<p>
Consider <m>f(x,y) = e^{-r^4}</m> where <m>r = \sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>.
Compute its directional derivative at <m>(0,0)</m> with respect to the
unit vectors in (polar) directions
<m>\theta = 0, \dfrac{\pi}{4}, \dfrac{\pi}{2}</m>. What about any
other angle?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The function is symmetric with respect to rotations around the origin,
so all the directional derivatives will be equal. Therefore it is
sufficient to compute the derivative for <m>\theta = 0</m>, i.e.
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m>:
<md>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
= \frac{\partial}{\partial x} e^{-(x^2+y^2)^2}
= -2(x^2+y^2)\cdot 2x\, e^{-(x^2+y^2)^2}.
</md>
Plugging in <m>x = y = 0</m> we see that this is zero. Hence the
directional derivative at <m>(0,0)</m> is <m>0</m> in the directions
<m>\theta = 0, \dfrac{\pi}{4}, \dfrac{\pi}{2}</m>, and by the same
symmetry it is <m>0</m> for every other angle as well.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-gr-2">
<statement>
<p>
Find all points at which the direction of fastest change of the
function <m>f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2 - 2x - 4y</m> is
<m>\langle 1, 1\rangle</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The direction of fastest change is
<m>\nabla f(x,y) = \langle 2x-2,\, 2y-4\rangle</m>, so we need to
find all points <m>(x,y)</m> such that
<m>\nabla f(x,y) = k\langle 1,1\rangle</m>. Then
<md>
2x - 2 = k \quad\text{and}\quad 2y - 4 = k,
</md>
so
<md>
2x - 2 = 2y - 4 \;\Rightarrow\; y = x + 1.
</md>
Hence, the direction of fastest change is <m>\langle 1,1\rangle</m>
at all points on the line <m>y = x + 1</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-rev-gr-line"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rev-gr-line">
<caption>
Level curves of <m>f(x,y) = x^2+y^2-2x-4y</m>, which are circles
centered at <m>(1,2)</m>. Along the line <m>y = x+1</m> the gradient
<m>\nabla f</m> points in the direction <m>\langle 1,1\rangle</m>.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rev-gr-line">
<diagram dimensions="(340,320)" margins="12">
<definition>c1(t) = (1 + 0.8*cos(t), 2 + 0.8*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>c2(t) = (1 + 1.6*cos(t), 2 + 1.6*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>c3(t) = (1 + 2.4*cos(t), 2 + 2.4*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-2.1,-1.1,4.3,5.1)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="gr-c1" function="c1" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="gr-c2" function="c2" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="gr-c3" function="c3" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
<line at="gr-line" endpoints="((-1.9,-0.9),(3.9,4.9))"
      stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="3"/>
<point at="gr-center" p="(1,2)" fill="#7F7F7F"/>
<vector at="gr-v1" v="(0.75,0.75)" tail="(1.566,2.566)"
        stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="gr-v2" v="(0.75,0.75)" tail="(2.131,3.131)"
        stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="gr-v3" v="(0.75,0.75)" tail="(2.697,3.697)"
        stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<label at="gr-line-lab" anchor="(-1.05,-0.95)" alignment="e" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>y=x+1</m>
</label>
<label at="gr-grad-lab" anchor="(2.30,-0.78)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>\nabla f \parallel \langle 1,1\rangle</m>
</label>
<label at="gr-center-lab" anchor="(1.12,1.82)" alignment="se" color="#7F7F7F">
<m>(1,2)</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Three concentric blue circles centered at the point one comma two are level curves of f. A dark red line with slope one, y equals x plus one, passes through the center. At three points on this line outside the smallest circle, orange arrows of equal length point up and to the right in the direction one comma one, perpendicular to the circles.">
  <annotation ref="gr-c1" text="A level curve of f: a circle centered at one comma two."/>
  <annotation ref="gr-line" text="The line y equals x plus one, through the center of the circles, where the gradient points in the direction one comma one."/>
  <annotation ref="gr-v1" text="The gradient direction one comma one at a point on the line, perpendicular to the level curves."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-gr-3">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="yes">
<p>
If <m>\vec u = \langle a, b\rangle</m> is a unit vector and <m>f</m>
has continuous second partials, then
<md>
D^2_{\vec u} f(x,y) = f_{xx}a^2 + 2f_{xy}ab + f_{yy}b^2,
</md>
where <m>D^2_{\vec u} f = D_{\vec u}\left[D_{\vec u} f(x,y)\right]</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
We have that
<md>
D^2_{\vec u} f
= D_{\vec u}\left[D_{\vec u} f(x,y)\right]
= D_{\vec u}\left[a f_x + b f_y\right].
</md>
This simplifies to
<md>
\langle a f_{xx} + b f_{xy},\; a f_{xy} + b f_{yy}\rangle
\cdot \langle a, b\rangle
= f_{xx}a^2 + 2f_{xy}ab + f_{yy}b^2.
</md>
Note we needed <m>f</m> to have continuous second partials to invoke
Clairaut's Theorem.
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-gr-1">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="yes">
<p>
<m>D_{\vec i + \vec j}\,f(x,y) = f_x + f_y</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
Since
<m>\vec i + \vec j = \langle 1, 1\rangle</m>,
<md>
D_{\vec i + \vec j}\,f(x,y)
= \nabla f(x,y) \cdot \langle 1, 1\rangle
= f_x + f_y.
</md>
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-gr-2">
<title>A mountain lion on a mountain</title>
<statement>
<p>
A mountain lion runs on a mountain whose height above the point
<m>(x,y)</m> is <m>z = x^2 + \sin^2(xy)</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
In which direction(s) should the mountain lion run from point
<m>(1, 0, 1)</m> so that the height is increasing at the fastest
possible rate? What is this fastest rate?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
In which direction(s) should the mountain lion run from point
<m>(1, 0, 1)</m> so that the height is increasing at <em>half</em> of
the fastest possible rate?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The fastest possible rate of increase is attained in the direction of
the gradient:
<md>
\nabla z = \left\langle \frac{\partial z}{\partial x},
\frac{\partial z}{\partial y}\right\rangle
= \left\langle 2x + 2\sin(xy)\cos(xy)\,y,\;
2\sin(xy)\cos(xy)\,x\right\rangle.
</md>
At <m>(1, 0, 1)</m>, this gradient is simply
<m>\nabla z(1,0) = \langle 2, 0\rangle</m>, and the fastest possible
rate of change is the length of the gradient, i.e.
<m>|\nabla z(1,0)| = |\langle 2,0\rangle| = 2</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Half of the fastest possible rate of height increase at point
<m>(1,0,1)</m> is <m>\frac12 \cdot 2 = 1</m>. Let
<m>\vec u = \langle a, b\rangle</m> be a unit vector in the desired
direction. Then the directional derivative
<m>D_{\vec u}(1,0) = 1</m> is the desired rate of change of height at
<m>(1,0,1)</m> in the direction of <m>\vec u</m>; it can be calculated
using the dot product with the gradient:
<md>
1 = D_{\vec u}(1,0)
= \vec u \cdot \nabla z(1,0)
= \langle a, b\rangle \cdot \langle 2, 0\rangle
= 2a + 0b = 2a.
</md>
Thus, <m>a = \frac12</m> and <m>b</m> must be such that
<m>\vec u = \left\langle \frac12, b\right\rangle</m> is unit, i.e.
<m>\left(\frac12\right)^2 + b^2 = 1</m>, from which
<m>b^2 = \frac34</m> and <m>b = \pm\frac{\sqrt3}{2}</m>. Therefore,
the desired directions in which the height is increasing at a rate
equal to half of the fastest possible rate are
<m>\vec u_1 = \left\langle \frac12, \frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right\rangle</m>
and
<m>\vec u_2 = \left\langle \frac12, -\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right\rangle</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</worksheet>
